From cf60a59a1a8ca8bd3d8497787b731e8b0a90ef2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:50:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] attendance: one register for the Robo line and the
 Chromia line
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The same operators work both lines — some days one, some days the other,
some days both — so attendance is one register, not two that have to be
kept in step. There is one set of tables, one handler and one screen; the
two ERPs differ only in which door they come through.

That is why nothing here synchronises anything. A day entered on the Robo
tablet IS the row the Chromia tablet shows, because it is the row, and
(date, operator) is UNIQUE in the database — so the same day saved from
both ends is one record the second save corrects, never a duplicate.

── THE DATA ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Three tables, namespaced attendance_* and owned by neither module, the way
consumable_* and fg_* are. Added to Neon out-of-band as usual, additively.
Its block sits between LAB and ACCOUNTS, under its own banner, because the
Accounts block has to stay at the foot of the schema and the Lab block ends
at the next module banner.
  • attendance_operator — the roster, shared. Deliberately NOT RoboOperator:
    that one is Robo-named and dormant (seeded once, read nowhere, no UI),
    and would read oddly as the source of Chromia's operators. Seeded with
    the two the owner named.
  • attendance_day — one row per operator per date, with the lines worked.
    An operator on both lines has ONE In and ONE Out: they came to work once.
  • attendance_leave — leave asked for, free of the attendance row, because
    leave is asked in advance and may never become a day worked.

── THE API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
One handler, mounted twice: /api/robo/attendance/… behind roboGate and the
middleware's /api/robo rule, /api/chromia/attendance/… behind chromiaGate
and the /api/chromia rule. NOT a shared /api/attendance prefix, which would
be wrong twice over — middleware has no rule for it, so every other
department's login would reach it, while the ROBO and CHROMIA role caps
(which allow only their own module's paths) would lock out the two logins
that need it. Twenty near-identical route files was the other option, and
navModel.ts already records what happens to hand-kept copies.

── THE PAGE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Last section of both ERPs, one component rendered by both pages.
  • Daily Attendance — a date, then the roster in alphabetical order with
    Line, In-Time, Out-Time, Total Working Hours and Edit. The lines are a
    two-way pick; the times are the entry form's own TimeInput, so 1625 is
    16:25 and 1367 is refused; the hours appear as the Out is typed, from
    the same function the server stores by, so the row does not change on
    save. A night shift crossing midnight is eight hours, not minus sixteen,
    and anything past sixteen hours reads as the typo it is. + Add Operator
    adds, renames and removes; an operator with attendance on record is
    taken off the roster rather than deleted, so their history survives.
  • Request Leave — empty to start, as asked: date, S.No., operator, leave
    time (from–to, or blank for a full day), reason, remarks. S.No. is the
    row's position within its date, computed and never stored — the rule
    Slab Records already follows, because a typed serial gets mistyped and
    then two rows claim the same number.
  • Downloads — record type, operator, and one period (a date, last week,
    month-wise, a custom range, or all records), combining as asked: an
    operator and a month gives that operator in that month. The results are
    shown on the page first and the Excel is built from the SAME query, so
    the sheet is the table you were just looking at.

Responsive: checked rendered at 390, 810 and 1440px — no sideways scroll,
44px tap targets on touch, both registers readable on a phone.

Nav: last row of ROBO_ITEMS and CHROMIA_ITEMS, a section in each tablet's
own arm (both are allowlists — a row left out is a page with no link), the
Chromia swipe order and its route constant. nav-sidebar.json moves with it:
four logins gain the row and nothing else differs, checked structurally
rather than by eye before the fixture was rewritten.

tests: the lines (one, both, order-independent, de-duplicated), worked
hours including midnight and the long-shift cap, leave times, alphabetical
names, every period mode (including February in a leap year and December
rolling into January), the filters combining, a half-filled range staying
open, the sheet's columns matching the register's, the file name, and
guards that both ERPs run the one handler over the one upsert, that both
pages render the one component, that the table and the sheet share a query,
and that Attendance is last in both navigations. Mutation-checked.

